{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Provide node2 with a persistent HTTP forward proxy on node1 TCP port 3128.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Squid 2.6.14 was installed and configured on node1, listened on TCP 3128, accepted a successful proxied request from node2, and returned after a demonstrated node1 reboot with the same working policy.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d9aebc2ee61944ed","cmd-4191c96324284a19","cmd-673da83e4e8c435e","cmd-3641d07db7854a3a","cmd-c199876bbee24698","cmd-1ccf35cbede44340","global:after_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Preserve the staged legacy origin on node2 and make its approved handbook reachable through the proxy on TCP port 8081.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The original staged script and handbook were observed before implementation; the executor added a separate SysV service without modifying the staged files. The origin listened on 8081 and served the expected handbook directly and through the proxy, including after both nodes rebooted.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node2","cmd-f5fc93fbc16647b9","cmd-23479c36fec34d0e","cmd-df7c36e293374b10","cmd-93f55e9d60aa45e5","cmd-72995ad7c6714fb4","cmd-3465e860868a4851"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Permit ordinary HTTP only to the approved node2 origin on port 8081, reject other destination ports, and reject CONNECT tunnels.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The active ACL restricts access by client, destination address, destination port, and method. Direct behavior showed the approved request returning the handbook, an HTTP request to port 8080 returning 403, and CONNECT to port 8081 returning 403. The same results were observed after both reboots and corroborated by Squid access records.","evidence_ids":["cmd-aa13632e20394bc5","cmd-4191c96324284a19","cmd-1ccf35cbede44340","cmd-72995ad7c6714fb4","cmd-ec5cd50b897d4988"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on Ubuntu 7.10 with kernel 2.6.22-14-generic after implementation and reboot. The proxy package was the Ubuntu 7.10 Squid 2.6.14 package and operated successfully on that platform.","evidence_ids":["cmd-be72d3b70bca4fde","cmd-f5fc93fbc16647b9","cmd-22efda4c7e4e41c6","cmd-4191c96324284a19","cmd-ec5cd50b897d4988","cmd-3465e860868a4851","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.96,"summary":"Mutations were causally connected to installing/configuring Squid, making the staged origin persistent, gathering bounded evidence, or executing the mandatory restart protocol. The temporary /etc/hosts repository pin was restored before reboot. The failed initial Squid configuration was directly corrected and the recovered service was verified. The only unwanted retained residue was /tmp/denied-body on node2, created or overwritten by the denial-test commands and not cleaned up; it remained in the final lifecycle snapshot. No damaged package state was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8f69b691719a403c","cmd-098aa22e3bb44a6d","cmd-43ea4aed37d24a22","cmd-673da83e4e8c435e","cmd-aa13632e20394bc5","cmd-1ccf35cbede44340","cmd-72995ad7c6714fb4","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The proxy and origin worked with the required allow/deny behavior, survived sequential reboots, and retained the legacy OS and kernel. The mandatory restart protocol was complete for both nodes: each had a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered service/data/path behavior, independently corroborated by lifecycle boot observations. Hygiene was strong aside from one small temporary test artifact left on node2.","limitations":["No post-executor cryptographic checksum of serve.py was captured; preservation is supported by the complete command trace, the unchanged execution path, and successful recovered behavior rather than a final checksum comparison.","Behavioral denial testing sampled port 8080 and CONNECT to port 8081; rejection of every other port and every CONNECT target is additionally supported by the active deny-by-default ACL configuration rather than exhaustive probing.","Lifecycle failed-service inspection was unavailable because these legacy nodes do not provide systemctl; service health was instead demonstrated through process, listener, request, reboot, and recovery evidence."]}
